<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Someone's Home</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>1.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>1</string>

	<!-- CARRIED FROM THE STACK-GATE SPIKE, WHERE ITS ABSENCE WOULD HAVE FALSIFIED THE RESULT.
	     iOS caps an app at 60 fps on a ProMotion panel unless it opts in here. At 60 Hz the
	     frame interval is 16.7 ms instead of 8.33 ms, so every latency figure gets twice the
	     budget it should have.

	     That matters more here than it did there. The lamp must go dark in the SAME FRAME as phone
	     contact — it is the entire mitigation for losing the anonymous revoke — and a frame
	     budget silently doubled is a blackout that looks fast in testing and is not.

	     The key is ...OnPhone. The shorter CADisableMinimumFrameDuration is not a real key and
	     is ignored without complaint. -->
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>

	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string></string>
	</dict>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Portrait only. The phone is a lamp held in one hand, and a rotation would relayout the
	     panel mid-round — a light level nobody authored, which rule 5 forbids. -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>

	<!-- The panel IS the interface, edge to edge. A system status bar over it would sit on top
	     of the device design's own status row, which D-078 requires on every screen. -->
	<key>UIStatusBarHidden</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- D1: the host embeds a server and every phone connects over the local network. iOS gates
	     that behind a user prompt, and this string is what the prompt says. The architecture's
	     warning stands: the prompt must be warmed during setup, in the light -- never at arming
	     in a dark room. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>The house connects the players' phones to the host's phone over this network. Nothing leaves the room.</string>

	<!-- D2: the front camera reads the printed cards, and iOS stops an app that touches a camera
	     without this string rather than refusing the request. The wording says WHAT IS READ and
	     WHERE IT GOES, because the sentence is read by somebody who is about to hand a phone to
	     seven friends: the camera reads the symbols on the cards, and no picture is kept.

	     Same warning as the local-network prompt above, and it is the more serious one here: iOS
	     asks ONCE EVER, and the scan screen is a screen a player is on in a dark house. The prompt
	     must be warmed during setup, in the light. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>The house reads the printed cards through the camera. No picture is taken, kept or sent anywhere.</string>

	<!-- The house reads whether a phone is being carried, which is the motion budget the
	     architecture accumulates client-side. iOS STOPS an app that raises this prompt with no
	     string rather than refusing the request -- so the absence of this key is not a permission
	     bug, it is the app failing in the host's hand in front of the whole party.

	     D-161: this is asked at the Perms screen, in the light, with everything else. -->
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>The house notices when a phone is being carried from room to room. Nothing about you leaves this house.</string>

	<!-- The proximity channel: which phones are near each other, so the house can place people
	     without a camera in every room. Same fatal-if-absent rule as the motion string above, and
	     the ALWAYS spelling is the one iOS reads on every version this project targets. -->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>The house senses which phones are near each other in the rooms. No connection leaves this house.</string>

	<!-- Nearby Interaction (UWB), which measures the distance for contact, report and presence
	     while Bluetooth picks which phones to pair (D-300, D-301, D-306, D-308). iOS ends an
	     app that runs an NISession without this string,
	     so it ships in every configuration. The wording is the owner's (draft B, approved
	     2026-11-18). Until the 2026-11-18 build it was injected into Debug and Playtest only, for
	     the UWB spike. -->
	<key>NSNearbyInteractionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Someone's Home measures the distance between phones in the room. It is how the game sees who is close to whom.</string>

	<!-- Without this list, browsing for the host's service silently finds NOTHING on iOS 14+ --
	     no error, no prompt, an empty result forever. A fail-closed OS behaviour worth a comment
	     precisely because the failure has no symptom. -->
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_someones-home._tcp</string>
	</array>

	<!-- So a host can pull a house map or a recording off the device without a cable. The map's
	     stored form is its export form by construction (game-architecture.md:419). -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
